{"causes": ["The Abbasid Caliphate's policy of intellectual patronage fostered an environment for scientific translation and innovation in Baghdad.", "The arrival of Indian scholars and texts, notably the *Brahmasphuṭasiddhānta*, introduced sophisticated astronomical methods to the Islamic world.", "The existing Persian and Hellenistic scientific traditions provided a rich intellectual foundation for the assimilation of new knowledge."], "effects": ["Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq's work facilitated the widespread adoption of Indian numerals and computational methods in the Islamic world.", "His *Kitāb al-Zīj ʿalā Daraj al-Sindhind* became a foundational text for subsequent Islamic astronomical studies and zīj compilations.", "The transmission of the *Sindhind* through his efforts directly influenced the development of trigonometry and planetary models by later Islamic scholars."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "abbasid-caliphate", "sourceName": "Abbasid Caliphate", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "yaʿqūb-ibn-ṭāriq", "targetName": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq", "context": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq was an active scholar and mathematician within the flourishing intellectual environment of the early Abbasid Caliphate."}, {"sourceSlug": "yaʿqūb-ibn-ṭāriq", "sourceName": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "brahmasphutasiddhanta", "targetName": "Brahmasphuṭasiddhānta", "context": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq played a crucial role in transmitting and adapting the Indian astronomical treatise *Brahmasphuṭasiddhānta* into Arabic around 770 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "baghdad", "sourceName": "Baghdad", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "yaʿqūb-ibn-ṭāriq", "targetName": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq", "context": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq conducted his primary astronomical and mathematical work in Baghdad, the intellectual center of the early Abbasid Caliphate."}, {"sourceSlug": "yaʿqūb-ibn-ṭāriq", "sourceName": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "al-khwarizmi", "targetName": "Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi", "context": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq's work on the *Sindhind* tradition directly influenced later astronomers like al-Khwarizmi, who also compiled his own *Sindhind* zīj."}, {"sourceSlug": "yaʿqūb-ibn-ṭāriq", "sourceName": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "kitab-al-zij-ala-daraj-al-sindhind", "targetName": "Kitāb al-Zīj ʿalā Daraj al-Sindhind", "context": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq authored the *Kitāb al-Zīj ʿalā Daraj al-Sindhind*, a foundational astronomical handbook based on Indian methods, around 770 CE."}], "places": [{"name": "Baghdad, Iraq", "role": "Center of Activity"}, {"name": "Persia", "role": "Place of Origin"}, {"name": "Ujjain, India", "role": "Source of Knowledge"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "yaʿqūb-ibn-ṭāriq"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q527284", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ya%27qub_ibn_Tariq"], "tags": ["Logicians & Mathematicians", "innovation-and-technology", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq was a crucial conduit for the transmission of Indian astronomical and mathematical knowledge to the Islamic world, directly influencing the development of sophisticated zīj tables and computational methods for centuries. His work laid foundational elements for later scientific breakthroughs in the region.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-24T21:40:08Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32777969874", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq was a Persian astronomer and mathematician who lived in Baghdad.", "newValue": "Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq, a prominent Persian astronomer and mathematician, was a pivotal figure in the early Abbasid scientific renaissance, flourishing in Baghdad during the latter half of the 8th century CE until his death in 796 CE. His work was instrumental in the intellectual ferment that characterized the court of Caliph al-Mansur, particularly through his role in transmitting and adapting Indian astronomical knowledge into the nascent Islamic scientific tradition. He stands as a foundational bridge between the sophisticated astronomical methods of ancient India and the burgeoning mathematical and observational sciences of the Islamic Golden Age.

His most significant contribution was his involvement in the adaptation of the *Brahmasphuṭasiddhānta*, an Indian astronomical treatise by Brahmagupta, which arrived in Baghdad around 770 CE. Working alongside scholars like Ibrāhīm al-Fazārī, Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq produced the *Kitāb al-Zīj ʿalā Daraj al-Sindhind*, a comprehensive astronomical handbook (zīj) based on the Indian *Sindhind* methods. This work introduced concepts such as the sine function, new methods for calculating planetary positions, and the Indian system of numeration, including the use of zero, to the Islamic world, thereby laying critical groundwork for future mathematical and astronomical advancements.

The transmission of the *Sindhind* via Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq profoundly influenced subsequent generations of Islamic astronomers and mathematicians. His efforts, alongside those of his contemporaries, ensured that sophisticated Indian computational techniques and models became integral to the development of Islamic astronomy, leading to innovations such as the construction of advanced observatories and the refinement of astronomical tables. This cross-cultural intellectual exchange directly paved the way for later giants like al-Khwarizmi and al-Battani."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-24T21:40:08Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32777969874", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "800 CE, Baghdad"}, {"title": "Accumulation of mathematical knowledge and formal systems", "type": "Idea", "year": "800 CE, Baghdad"}], "newValue": ["The Abbasid Caliphate's policy of intellectual patronage fostered an environment for scientific translation and innovation in Baghdad.", "The arrival of Indian scholars and texts, notably the *Brahmasphuṭasiddhānta*, introduced sophisticated astronomical methods to the Islamic world.", "The existing Persian and Hellenistic scientific traditions provided a rich intellectual foundation for the assimilation of new knowledge."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-24T21:40:08Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32777969874", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Advanced mathematical knowledge used by subsequent scholars", "type": "Idea", "year": "796 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "796 CE"}], "newValue": ["Yaʿqūb ibn Ṭāriq's work facilitated the widespread adoption of Indian numerals and computational methods in the Islamic world.", "His *Kitāb al-Zīj ʿalā Daraj al-Sindhind* became a